WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.000
Hey guys, like many of you, we're sheltering
in place.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:10.240
In fact, we're even separated from each other
while Julia awaits the results of her COVID test.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.080
Thankfully, I'm feeling much better, but I don't wanna get
Philip or the baby sick.

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:18.750
But we know that people are looking for financial
info more than ever, so we're gonna do this

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:24.020
lo-fi, and try to answer some of the pressing
questions on top of people's minds, starting

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:25.620
with... the stock market crash.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:31.180
I'm gonna let Philip handle this one and keep
getting rest, but I will be back next week!

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:34.980
Remember way back in February 2020?

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:40.420
Ah, the good old days -- stock market at all
time highs, everyone had a job, all the toilet

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:41.840
paper you could ever want.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:42.980
Life was great.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:49.120
The US stock market, measured by the DOW,
has fallen over 35% in a month's time, wiping

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.190
away years of growth.

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:56.430
We witnessed the worst single-day decline
since 1987, and the exchanges were temporarily

00:00:56.430 --> 00:01:00.670
shut down three times in a week when
it fell too far in a single day.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:06.470
When the stock market fell by more than 20%,
we officially entered a “bear market”.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:09.780
Bear markets usually - but not always - precede
a recession.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:15.159
Like the stock market crashes preceding the
Great Depression of 1929 or the Great Recession

00:01:15.159 --> 00:01:16.479
of 2008.

00:01:16.479 --> 00:01:21.740
Depending on the criteria, there have been
between 10 and 20 bear markets in the last

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:25.000
century, and this month, we just entered another
one.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.560
This is a scary time we’re living in right
now, I’m the first to admit it.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:32.909
I was worried when Julia came down with a
bad cough and had to get tested for COVID-19.

00:01:32.909 --> 00:01:38.850
I am hurting for my friends and family who
are experiencing layoffs, slowdowns and closures.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:44.310
This will have a lasting, harmful effect on
our economy, and many people’s lives.

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:49.579
But what we’re seeing right now in the markets
is not unprecedented.

00:01:49.579 --> 00:01:54.950
In the words of investing pioneer Sir John
Templeton “The four most expensive words

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:59.219
in the English Language are ‘this time it’s
different.’“

00:01:59.219 --> 00:02:02.649
We always think the current crisis is unique.

00:02:02.649 --> 00:02:08.190
World Wars, nuclear meltdowns, Great Depressions
-- even the global Spanish Flu Pandemic

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:10.640
that left 50 million dead.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:16.060
The stock market’s long-term average is
accounting for every one of those terrible times.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:18.820
Every time, things seem hopeless.

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:20.670
Every time, markets recover.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:24.170
The bear wanders back into the woods, and
markets thrive again.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:28.459
It will happen with coronavirus, and the other
bears in our future too.

00:02:28.459 --> 00:02:34.239
Selling out of stock investments when a bear
market rears its head turns a bad situation

00:02:34.239 --> 00:02:37.799
into a worse situation in two big ways.

00:02:37.799 --> 00:02:44.040
First, you “realize the loss”, turning
a decline in share value into a real loss

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:45.360
in your bank account.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:50.940
Think of it like seeing your home appraised
for less, and immediately putting it up for sale.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:52.400
You’d never do that!

00:02:52.409 --> 00:02:57.709
Second, you’re making nearly certain that
you miss the recovery when it happens.

00:02:57.709 --> 00:03:04.150
Between 1993 and 2013, the S&amp;P 500 grew by
9.2% annually.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:10.019
But if you take out just the 10 best trading
days over that period, your annual average is cut

00:03:10.019 --> 00:03:12.069
nearly in half.

00:03:12.069 --> 00:03:18.100
If you missed the 60 best trading days, you’d
average a -4.39% return!

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:24.569
By jumping out after a bear arrives, you seriously
risk missing the wave of the recovery.

00:03:24.569 --> 00:03:29.959
The aggressive among you may be wondering,
“Well, is now the time to BUY stocks?”

00:03:29.959 --> 00:03:33.220
Maybe… but with a few caveats.

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:37.640
Financial experts discourage investing in
the stock market with any money you may need

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:39.909
sooner than 10 years from now.

00:03:39.909 --> 00:03:45.370
Keep money for short-term needs in safe havens
like cash, bonds, or CD’s.

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:50.109
But if you have a nice cash cushion AND you
were already planning to invest that money

00:03:50.109 --> 00:03:52.019
anyway -- yes!

00:03:52.019 --> 00:03:55.260
Go ahead and continue to invest into the market.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:00.829
By investing in good times and bad, you get
to take advantage of “dollar-cost averaging”,

00:04:00.829 --> 00:04:06.530
a strategy that, by investing on a regular
basis, allows you to buy more shares on the

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:10.180
cheap and less shares as they get expensive.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:12.099
Investing is for the long-term.

00:04:12.099 --> 00:04:16.900
Retirement or college accounts are designed
to be invested and grow for decades.

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:22.819
Don’t let the turbulent news of today make
you rethink a solid long-term strategy.

00:04:22.819 --> 00:04:27.850
That big mean bear will eventually wander
back home and you’ll be glad you kept your

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:30.270
cool and didn’t make him mad.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:33.650
And that’s my Two Cents.

